Interview One-Liner:

NodeClaim is the lifecycle object connecting Karpenter's scheduling decision to real infrastructure.

6. How does consolidation work?

Answer:

Consolidation removes underutilized nodes.

Example:

Plain Text
Node1 = 15%
Node2 = 20%
Node3 = 10%

Karpenter may move workloads:

Plain Text
Node1 = 45%

and terminate Node2 and Node3.

Benefits:

Cost reduction
Better utilization
7. What is Drift Detection?

Answer:

Drift occurs when an existing node differs from the desired configuration.

Example:

Current nodes:

Plain Text
AMI-v1

Desired:

Plain Text
AMI-v2

Karpenter detects the drift and replaces outdated nodes.

Other drift triggers:

IAM role changes
Security group changes
AMI changes

Interview One-Liner:

Drift detection ensures running nodes match the desired NodePool and NodeClass configuration.

