5. Pending Pods Even Though Karpenter Is Installed

This is an extremely common troubleshooting scenario.

Step 1

Check pod:

Shell
kubectl describe pod <pod>

Look for:

Plain Text
Insufficient CPU
Insufficient Memory
Node affinity conflict
Taint conflict
Step 2

Check Karpenter logs

Shell
kubectl logs -n karpenter deploy/karpenter

Common errors:

Plain Text
AccessDenied
Subnet not found
Security group not found
No instance type matched
Step 3

Check NodeClaims

Shell
kubectl get nodeclaims

If missing:

Plain Text
Scheduling logic problem

If present but not Ready:

Plain Text
AWS provisioning issue
 
Step 4

Check AWS tags

Karpenter discovers:

Plain Text
Subnets
Security Groups

using tags.

Wrong tags:

Plain Text
No nodes created
Step 5

Check IAM

Very common.

Missing:

Plain Text
ec2:RunInstances
iam:PassRole
 

results in:

Plain Text
NodeClaim created
EC2 launch fails
Interview Answer

I would start with kubectl describe pod, review Karpenter controller logs, verify NodeClaims, check NodePool constraints, and then validate AWS subnet, security-group tags, and IAM permissions.

